What is in this repository
--------------------------

1. homr (vendor/homr, licence in vendor/HOMR-LICENSE)

   - Project: homr, an optical music recognition program by its
     authors and contributors. Upstream: https://github.com/liebharc/homr
   - Version: 0.7.0 (its Changelog's latest entry; its pyproject.toml carries a
     placeholder version, 0.1.0, set at build time).
   - Licence: GNU Affero General Public License 3.0 (homr's pyproject.toml says
     AGPL-3.0; its LICENSE file is vendor/HOMR-LICENSE, byte for byte as upstream,
     including the HTML-escaped "&lt;" and "&gt;" in its copyright line).
   - The Python files, tokenizer JSON files and everything else under
     vendor/homr/ are an unedited copy of homr's `homr/` package. Nothing in them
     was changed. The one thing left out is homr's segmentation model file
     (see "Segmentation model" below).
   - homr itself builds on the segmentation networks of oemer
     (https://github.com/BreezeWhite/oemer) and the transformer of
     Polyphonic-TrOMR (https://github.com/NetEase/Polyphonic-TrOMR); see homr's
     own README for their credits.

2. Ours (AGPL-3.0, same licence)

   - nawtara-reader (launcher script)
   - nawtara_reader/cli.py      the command line, output files, exit codes
   - nawtara_reader/page.py     the changes made to homr at run time (below),
                                and the per-bar figures written to conf.json
   - nawtara_reader/onnx_loop.py  the staff reader: an onnxruntime decoding loop
   - nawtara_reader/layout.py   where each bar is on the page image (layout.json)
   - requirements.txt, README.md, NOTICE

What is not in this repository
------------------------------

The trained staff model: `encoder.onnx` and `decoder_fused.onnx`. It is our own
model and does not contain homr's transformer weights. The files are not
published here. The command needs them: give the folder holding them in the environment
variable NAWTARA_READER_MODELS (or with --models, or --encoder and --decoder).
Without them the command stops with exit code 2.

Segmentation model
------------------

homr 0.7.0 ships its staff-segmentation network inside its git repository as
`homr/segmentation/segnet_308-3296ccd40960f90ca6ab9c035cca945675d30a0f.onnx`
(about 55 MB). It is not downloaded at run time in this version. Its licence is
not stated separately: it sits in homr's AGPL-3.0 repository, and homr's README
says its segmentation models come from oemer. We have not confirmed upstream's
position on redistributing the weights, so we do not redistribute that file
here. Take it from the homr repository at the
0.7.0 release (same path) and put it in vendor/homr/segmentation/, or name your
copy with NAWTARA_READER_SEGNET.

Every change made to homr's behaviour at run time
-------------------------------------------------

All of these are made when the program starts, in nawtara_reader/page.py and
nawtara_reader/cli.py, by replacing functions or table entries of the
imported homr modules. homr's files are not edited. Checked against the code.

 1. Vocabulary: the lift list gains `qb` (quarter-tone flat) and `q#`
    (quarter-tone sharp); the rhythm list gains the token `keySign`; a
    `keySign` token counts as having a staff position.
 2. The staff reader: homr's `Staff2Score` model call is replaced
    (`cli.py` sets `SPT.inference`) by `OnnxStaffReader`, which runs the model
    on onnxruntime.
 3. Confidence: for each chosen symbol the probability of the top class of the
    rhythm, pitch and lift outputs is kept (the minimum of the three for notes,
    the rhythm probability otherwise). For a note that carries an accidental
    sign, the lift probability is also kept on its own (`minSignProb`). They
    are only reported in conf.json.
 4. `LIFT_TO_ALTER` gains `qb` = -0.5 and `q#` = +0.5, so quarter tones are
    written as <alter>-0.5 / <alter>0.5 in the MusicXML.
 5. Key signatures: a run of `keySign` symbols is collapsed into one key
    signature, written as <key-step>/<key-alter> pairs; a numeric code is
    written as <fifths>; an empty one as <fifths>0. homr's `build_key` is
    replaced for this.
 6. Per-bar figures: `build_measures` is wrapped to record, for every bar of
    part 1, the lowest symbol probability. This does not change the MusicXML.
 7. Staff pairs (default `--staves top`): where homr's cutter joins a braced
    or bracketed pair of staves (`Staff.merge`, `find_braces_brackets_and_grand_staff_lines`
    are wrapped), only the UPPER staff is given to the model. On some pages two
    neighbouring single staves are also treated as a pair. With `--staves merge` homr's own joining is used instead.
 8. Threads: onnxruntime sessions that homr opens without options (its
    segmentation net) get the thread count given with --threads (default 2),
    and so do OpenCV and the OMP/OpenBLAS/MKL/Accelerate thread variables.
 9. Network and extras: `requests` and `rapidocr` are replaced by stubs, and
    homr's `download_file` raises, so nothing is downloaded and no title OCR is
    possible. `detect_title` returns an empty title. The teaser image writer
    is switched off. homr's log (which prints the whole reading) is silenced
    unless --verbose is given.
10. Segmentation model path: NAWTARA_READER_SEGNET, if set, overrides where
    homr looks for its segmentation model.
11. homr is run with its processing options all off (no debug images, no cache,
    no staff position files, all staves, no GPU) on a link to the image placed
    in a temporary folder inside the output folder, which is removed afterwards.
12. The launcher and cli.py refuse to finish (exit code 4) if PyTorch got
    imported; this program is meant to run without it.
13. Several pages in one run: homr opens a new session of its segmentation
    net for every page (`Segnet` in homr/segmentation/inference_segnet.py);
    the first session is kept and used for every page of the run. Each page
    is still handed to homr on its own, as in 11.
14. Bar positions (nawtara_reader/layout.py): these homr functions are wrapped
    to record what they are given and what they return, and hand every value
    through unchanged: `autocrop` and `detect_bar_lines` (homr.main),
    `prepare_staff_image`, `crop_image_and_return_new_top` and
    `center_image_on_canvas` (homr.staff_parsing), `build_measures`
    (homr.music_xml_generator). The decoder session is wrapped to keep its
    `attention` output, and the staff reader's `predict` to tag each symbol
    with its staff. Nothing in page.musicxml or conf.json depends on it.
15. Time signatures read (0.4.0): homr's clean-up of a staff's symbols
    (`remove_duplicated_symbols`, as homr.staff_parsing calls it) is wrapped.
    homr's own result is what the rest of the program gets, unchanged. On a
    copy of the symbols taken before it, the clean-up is run a second time
    with one rule changed: a time signature equal to the last one kept is
    kept and marked instead of removed. The bars of that copy are counted
    the way homr's writer counts them, and every time signature of part 1 is
    reported in conf.json (`signatures`). page.musicxml does not change.
16. Per-note lift distribution (0.5.0): the staff reader's decode loop also
    keeps the softmax of the lift branch at each step (classes under 0.005
    left out); the choice is still its arg max. conf.json lists it per note
    of part 1 (`bars[].notes`). page.musicxml does not change.
17. Page scale (0.5.1): before homr's segmentation, an image whose height is
    under a quarter of its width gets a white margin (one image height above
    and below, 5 % at the sides), and an image whose staff spacing would fall
    under 12 px at homr's 1920 px width is resized so it is 20 px instead (at
    most 6000 px wide). Other images go through homr's resize unchanged
    (`nawtara_reader/page_scale.py`, installed from page.setup()).
18. Staff window (0.5.2): homr cuts each staff between the staff above and
    the staff below (`staff_parsing._calculate_region`, wrapped). When two
    staves it found overlap, that window could close to a pixel and the page
    stopped in OpenCV's resize. The window now always keeps the staff's own
    lines plus one staff space above and below; a window that already does
    is unchanged (`nawtara_reader/staff_region.py`, installed from
    page.setup()).
19. Dark borders (0.5.3): after homr's autocrop (`homr.main.autocrop`,
    wrapped), the dark bands at the edges of the image are cut off: rows,
    then columns, outside the first and last that are mostly paper (half of
    them above 180 grey), when such a band is dark (mean under 60), at least
    3 % of the image, and the page inside it is paper (median above 180).
    The result is a view of the image, so layout.json maps boxes back to the
    upload. An image without such a band is unchanged
    (`nawtara_reader/border_trim.py`, installed from page.setup()).
